+### Vector batch resonance at 1,000,000 pairs
+
+This run extends the PR #35 study to one million pairs, the scale of a large
+recall batch. It ran on Linux x86_64, AMD Ryzen 7 7730U with AVX2, Zulu 27+35,
+with `DoubleVector.SPECIES_PREFERRED` length `4`.
+
+```bash
+./gradlew :monada-neuron-evaluation:jmh \
+  -PjmhArgs="-bm avgt -f 1 -wi 0 -i 1 -r 1s -p batchSize=1000000 \
+  ScalarResonanceBenchmark.benchmark(Scalar|Vector)BatchSoA"
+```
+
+| Pairs | Scalar (ns/op) | Vector (ns/op) | Speedup |
+| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
+| 1,000,000 | 61,480,212.0 | 6,907,889.0 | 8.9x |
+
+Vector is 8.9x faster than Scalar at one million pairs. The gain comes from
+the four 64-bit lanes of the AVX2 registers, which score four pairs per
+instruction, and it grows with batch size. Because resonance scoring is the
+dominant per-signal cost, enabling the Vector backend makes a cognitive cycle
+roughly 8x faster on this hardware.
+
